%[text] %[text:anchor:T_48B9CDC7] # Color Segmentation using Programmatic Approaches
%[text] Although the Color Thresholder App is quite powerful, it requires you to click and point at things in the image. This can make the approach a little less reproducible, especially if there is inconsistent coloring in your images. Sometimes a programmatic approach, one that relies on the properties of the image itself, can be more reproducible.
%[text] In this script, we will review strategies to automate color segmentation without manually tracing or clicking on objects of interest. 
%[text] 
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:T_969DBF63] ## Strawberries, redux
%[text] Let's start with the strawberries. 
%[text] ### Load Image
%[text] Here, we load the strawberry image and create a new structure, `p`, to organize our images.
clearvars
close all

mmSetUnitDataFolder(2) % change folder to unit2 %[output:0fb379a0]
p.name = 'strawberries.png'; % create a structure p (p for pic or photo)
p.rgb = imread(p.name); % add image to structure
imshow(p.rgb) %[output:1dd05d3f]
%%
%[text] ### Automating the segmentation
%[text] If you don't want to manually circle or click on strawberries (if say, you had hundreds of strawberry images to deal with), what should be your approach? 
%[text] The answer is that you should change the image to a colorspace that is better suited to handle colors mathematically and then perform the standard binarization steps on one or multiple channels in the converted image. The LAB colorspace was designed to handle colors mathematically. So, let's start there.
%[text] #### Convert to L\*a\*b\* space
%[text] Here we convert the RGB image to a L\*a\*b\* space using the function **rgb2lab**
p.lab = rgb2lab(p.rgb); % convert to L*a*b*
class(p.lab) % notice the class has changed from uint8 to double %[output:6e085fbc]
%[text] - In Lab colorspace, colors are mathematically separated from each other in geometric space \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A1D4EB69] #### Display the L\*a\*b\* channels
%[text] Here we display all three L\*a\*b\* channels and their histograms using a for loop.
figure; %[output:6833a41d]
tiledlayout(2,3); %[output:6833a41d]
p.titles = ["L (lightness)", " a (green-red)", "b (yellow-blue)"];

for n=1:3 %[output:group:573730ea]
    nexttile(n); %[output:6833a41d]
    imshow(p.lab(:,:,n),[]) % index out the channel and display
    title(p.titles(n));

    nexttile(n+3)
    histogram(p.lab(:,:,n))
end %[output:group:573730ea]
%[text] Notice that the strawberries really pop out in the a\* plane. This makes sense, because green and red are on opposite ends of the a\* plane. In terms of color opponency, the a\* plane represents the battle between the red and green forces in our minds.
%[text] To capture the strawberries, maybe we can just threshold the a\* plane using `imbinarize`.
%%
%[text] #### First, consider the values in the plane
%[text] Before we can threshold the a\* plane, we have to consider the values in the plane. Notice that the values in the L\*a\*b\* planes are vastly different from RGB values. The class of `p.lab` is double, and:
%[text] - the L\* plane ranges from 0 to 100
%[text] - the a\* and b\* planes contain negative numbers, and here run roughly from -40 to 80.
%[text] - Many of the distributions are multimodal (multiple peaks in the histogram) \
%[text] #### Index out a\* and normalize
%[text] Since the a\* plane has a non-standard intensity range (roughly -40 to 80), we need to normalize the plane to 0-1 before we can pass it to `imbinarize`.
p.a = p.lab(:,:,2); % index out a* plane
p.aNorm = rescale(p.a); % normalize a-plane to the range 0-1

%[text] Before we binarize, notice what `imbinarize` is actually doing. It picks the threshold for you, using **Otsu's method**, which searches for the cut point that best separates the histogram into two groups. The function `graythresh` returns that same threshold so we can see it.
level = graythresh(p.aNorm) % the threshold imbinarize will use %[output:161e603d]

figure %[output:98a126db]
histogram(p.aNorm) %[output:98a126db]
xline(level,'r','LineWidth',2) % draw the threshold on the histogram %[output:98a126db]
title('a* histogram with the Otsu threshold') %[output:98a126db]
%[text] - Notice where the line falls: right in the valley between the two humps. That valley is the boundary between "leafy background" and "strawberry".
%[text] - **This is what makes the approach reproducible.** Nobody chose that number. The image chose it. Hand the same code a new strawberry photo and `graythresh` will find a new threshold appropriate to that photo. \
%%
%[text] #### Binarize the normalized plane
p.aMaskNorm = imbinarize(p.aNorm); % binarize normalized plane

figure %[output:3be3fca6]
mmShowBurnImage(p.rgb,p.aMaskNorm) % burn mask and display %[output:3be3fca6]
%[text] - Well, that was easy. One threshold on one channel did what took a lot of clicking in the App - because converting to L\*a\*b\* put the strawberries and the leaves at opposite ends of a single axis. \
%%
%[text] #### Clean up noise and watershed
%[text] - remove the small specks of noise, and the partial strawberry running off the edge of the image
%[text] - separate touching strawberries \
p.aMaskClean = bwareaopen(p.aMaskNorm,200); % remove small noise from image
p.aMaskClean = imclearborder(p.aMaskClean); % remove objects touching the image border
p.aWatershed = mmGetWatershed(p.aMaskClean, 5); % separate touching strawberries

figure %[output:45b70e5a]
mmShowBurnImage(p.rgb,p.aWatershed) %[output:45b70e5a]
%[text] - Are the strawberries separated? Compare this to the watershed result you got from the SAM mask back in X3. \
%%
%[text] #### Final Review
figure;
mmTightTiledLayout("flow") %[output:615941ef]
for n=["rgb" "aNorm" "aMaskNorm" "aMaskClean" "aWatershed"] %[output:group:8409f9b2]
    nexttile %[output:615941ef]
    imshow(p.(n))
    title(n)
end %[output:group:8409f9b2]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_SCOREGT] ### How good is it? Compare against the ground truth
%[text] In X3 we built a hand-drawn ground truth mask and used **Jaccard** and **Dice** to score the masks that came out of the Apps. That same ground truth is still sitting in the data folder, and this script just segmented the very same image - only this time with eight lines of code and no clicking at all.
%[text] So let's put both approaches on the same scale.
load('strawberry-mask-ground-truth.mat','maskGT'); % the hand-drawn mask from X3

fprintf('Jaccard: %.3f\n', jaccard(maskGT, p.aMaskClean)); %[output:48d88646]
fprintf('Dice:    %.3f\n', dice(maskGT, p.aMaskClean)); %[output:0bee6df1]

figure %[output:30d20218]
imshowpair(maskGT, p.aMaskClean) %[output:30d20218]
title('Magenta - a* mask only, Green - ground truth only, White - overlap') %[output:30d20218]
%[text] - Compare these numbers to the table you built in X3. How does one threshold on the a\* plane stack up against all that clicking in the Color Thresholder?
%[text] - Remember the trade-off: the App mask needed a human. This one needed a function. \
%%
rp = regionprops('table',p.aWatershed,'basic') %[output:46ee40ea]
%%
%[text] - Why did we get more strawberries than we counted in X3? \
%[text] Look at the `Area` column. Most blobs are in the thousands of pixels, but a couple are tiny. Those are noise specks that survived `bwareaopen`, and the watershed happily split a few berries in two as well. Counting blobs is easy; counting *objects* means deciding what counts as an object.
fprintf('blobs found: %d\n', height(rp));
fprintf('blobs larger than 2000 px: %d\n', sum(rp.Area > 2000));
%[text] - Filtering on area gets us much closer to the number you get by eye. \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_73C70A21] ## Change Strawberry colors using HSV
%[text] Can we use HSV to change the color of the strawberries? We already have a good mask of the strawberries. We can use that mask to pull out just the Hue values that belong to strawberries, find the range those hues occupy, and then replace every hue in that range with a different one.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7EBA99BD] ### Convert to HSV
%[text] In this step, we just convert the RGB image to HSV and display the histogram. 
p.hsv = rgb2hsv(p.rgb); % convert to hsv
p.h = imsplit(p.hsv); % with one output, imsplit returns just the first plane (hue)
histogram(p.h) %[output:072ec904]
%[text] - So, are there two peaks, or 3? \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D0A4E47D] ### Get strawberry hues
%[text] What are the Hue values in just Strawberries? We can use the strawberry mask to index out the hue values from the H plane
clf; %[output:43a3f869]
histogram(p.h(p.aMaskClean),100) % index the hue plane with the strawberry mask %[output:43a3f869]
%[text] So let's do what we just said: find the range of strawberry hues by taking the minimum and the maximum.
hueVals = p.h(p.aMaskClean); % just the hue values inside the strawberries
[min(hueVals) max(hueVals)] % the "range" of strawberry hues %[output:97e1486f]
%[text] - That says the strawberries span essentially the *entire* hue range, 0 to 1. Which is useless.
%[text] - But look back at the histogram. The values are not spread out at all - they are piled up at both ends. \
%[text] - hmm, we have peaks at the low end and high end of the hue plane.
%[text] - What's going on here? \
%[text] Remember, the hue plane is **circular**. Hue 0 and hue 1 are the same color: RED. So the strawberry hues are not spread across the whole range - they are clustered in one spot that happens to straddle the seam where the circle was cut open and laid flat. `min` and `max` cannot see that, which is why they gave us a nonsense answer.
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7A91D19B] ### Replace strawberry hues
%[text] No problem. Instead of one range, we use a logical OR to capture the LOW end and the HIGH end in a single mask. Read the histogram above to pick the two cut points.
%[text] - Everything at or above 0.9 (just before the seam)
%[text] - OR everything at or below 0.051 (just after the seam) \
% reset hsv
p.hsv = rgb2hsv(p.rgb); % reconvert to hsv
p.h = imsplit(p.hsv); % extract the h plane

la = p.h >= 0.9 | p.h <= 0.051; % capture both ends of the hue plane, where the strawberry reds fall

p.h(la) = 0.75; % replace strawberry hues with new hue
p.hsv(:,:,1) = p.h; % add the h plane back to hsv

% display results
figure %[output:7b261b04]
imshow(hsv2rgb(p.hsv)) % change back to rgb and show %[output:7b261b04]
%[text] - The strawberries are now purple, and the leaves are untouched - because we only changed hues inside the range the strawberries occupied. \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_NOTRED] ## Does it generalize? The "not red" strawberries
%[text] At the top of this script we claimed that a programmatic approach is more reproducible, *especially if there is inconsistent coloring in your images*. Let's actually test that claim on a second image.
clearvars
close all

p.rgb = imread("Not_red_strawberries.png");
figure %[output:6d2514e3]
imshow(p.rgb) %[output:6d2514e3]
%[text] - You see red strawberries on a plate. Almost everybody does. \
%%
p.lab = rgb2lab(p.rgb);

figure; %[output:22e1c8f2]
tiledlayout(2,3); %[output:22e1c8f2]
p.titles = ["L (lightness)", " a (green-red)", "b (yellow-blue)"];

for n=1:3 %[output:group:2bd13e69]
    nexttile(n); %[output:22e1c8f2]
    imshow(p.lab(:,:,n),[]) % index out the channel and display
    title(p.titles(n));

    nexttile(n+3)
    histogram(p.lab(:,:,n))
end %[output:group:2bd13e69]
%%
%[text] ### Ask the pixels instead of your eyes
%[text] Before we segment anything, let's check whether those strawberries are actually red. A red pixel should have more red in it than green or blue.
R = q.rgb(:,:,1); G = q.rgb(:,:,2); B = q.rgb(:,:,3);
fprintf('pixels where red is the strongest channel: %.1f%%\n', 100*mean(R>G & R>B,'all')); %[output:2835cca3]
%[text] - **Zero.** Not one pixel in that image is red-dominant. The photo was shot through a cyan filter, so the "red" you see is entirely manufactured by your visual system - the same color constancy that fooled you on the cube in X3.
%[text] - Your eyes are correcting for the filter. The pixel values are not. \
%%
%[text] ### Run the exact same pipeline
%[text] Now run the a\* threshold we just built, with nothing changed, and see what happens.
p.complementL = imcomplement(rescale(p.lab(:,:,1)));
figure; imshow(p.complementL ) %[output:079c3909]
%%
p.mask = imbinarize(p.complementL);
mmShowBurnImage(p.rgb, p.mask) %[output:6d0c97e9]
%%
q.a = q.lab(:,:,2);
fprintf('a* range here:  %.1f to %.1f\n', min(q.a,[],'all'), max(q.a,[],'all')); %[output:29bb9253]
fprintf('a* range before: -35.4 to 74.3\n'); %[output:3fa2d71b]

q.aNorm = rescale(q.a);
q.aMask = imbinarize(q.aNorm);

figure %[output:973c5d95]
mmShowBurnImage(q.rgb, q.aMask) %[output:973c5d95]
%[text] - The a\* plane barely makes it above zero here, so there is no valley for `graythresh` to find. `imbinarize` still returns *a* threshold - it always does - and the mask it produces is meaningless.
%[text] - This is the honest limit of color segmentation: it works when your object really does differ from its background **in color**. When it does not, no amount of clever thresholding will save you. \
%%
%[text] ## Where do we go from here?
%[text] Color worked beautifully on the first strawberry image because the berries and the leaves sit at opposite ends of the a\* axis. It failed completely on the second one, because the color difference simply is not in the data.
%[text] So what else can we segment on, when color is unavailable or unreliable?
%[text] In the next exercise we look at **texture** - how rough or smooth a region is - and use it to find a flatfish that is camouflaged to match the sand it is lying on. Exactly the case where color has nothing to offer. \\


